From: "T. Kurt Bond" <tkurtbond@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 13:37:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/48] Implement In.Name

It stopped the program with HALT(99), "Not implemented". It now reads
the characters up to the next blank or control character, Oakwood's
"name ... according to the file name format of the underlying operating
system (e.g. lib/My.Mod under Unix)", and sets Done to whether there was
one that fit in the array.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/runtime/In.Mod | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/runtime/In.Mod b/src/runtime/In.Mod
index fe9d89a6..b085fe4b 100644
--- a/src/runtime/In.Mod
+++ b/src/runtime/In.Mod
@@ -147,8 +147,17 @@
   END
 END String;
 
-PROCEDURE Name*(VAR name: ARRAY OF CHAR); (* Read filename. Presumably using shell semantics. *)
-BEGIN HALT(99) (* Not implemented *)
+(* Oakwood: "the name s at the current position according to the file name
+   format of the underlying operating system": the characters up to the next
+   blank or control character. A name too long for the array is not read. *)
+PROCEDURE Name*(VAR name: ARRAY OF CHAR);
+VAR i: INTEGER;
+BEGIN StartAndSkip; i := 0;
+  WHILE (readstate = ready) & (nextch > " ") & (i < LEN(name)-1) DO
+    name[i] := nextch; INC(i); ReadChar
+  END;
+  Done := (i > 0) & ((readstate # ready) OR (nextch <= " "));
+  IF Done THEN name[i] := 0X ELSE name[0] := 0X END
 END Name;
 
 (* Reads a real constant, [-] digit {digit} ["." {digit}] [("E" | "D")
